          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"There is no single settled symbolism for coin. Recognized readings include image, ownership, and allegiance in the tribute coin; a lost sinner or soul in Luke 15; material wealth, stewardship, betrayal, sacrificial giving, and providential provision in other contexts.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book unifies coin imagery anthropologically: the coin represents a person bearing an owner’s image, name, or mark, possessing an assigned value, undergoing refinement, and being sought when lost. The recovered coin specifically represents a repentant sinner.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"In the directly relevant established readings, a coin can represent a person or soul who bears God’s image and belongs to God, especially the lost sinner sought and recovered in Luke 15; elsewhere its meaning remains context-dependent.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A coin is an image-stamped and valued person belonging to an owner, with the lost and recovered coin representing a repentant sinner.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book combines the established image-and-ownership reading and lost-sinner reading into one general person-as-coin schema, then extends it to valuation, refinement, divine naming, and the beast’s mark. This reclassifies passages that consensus ordinarily treats as distinct motifs—wealth, giving, taxation, marks, or refined faith—under a unified anthropological identification.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction with the recognized image-bearer or lost-sinner interpretations. The material difference is that the book generalizes their shared personal referent across a wider set of texts, whereas the consensus cautions that coin symbolism is context-dependent and includes several independent meanings.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s central referent is not novel: the consensus explicitly recognizes both the coin as a lost sinner or soul and the analogy between a stamped coin and humanity bearing God’s image. It is not a simple match, however, because the book synthesizes these readings into a broader controlling category and incorporates cases not ordinarily identified as coin symbolism, especially the marks in Revelation and the refining of faith in 1 Peter. This changes the extension and organization of the established readings without replacing their central personal referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Luke 15 directly supports the recovered coin as a repentant sinner, and Matthew 22 supports image and ownership, but several other citations do not themselves identify a person as a coin. First Peter speaks of faith being tested like gold, Revelation concerns marks or names on people, and the legal valuation texts concern payments or assessments. The unified person-as-coin framework therefore depends on cross-textual analogy beyond what the printed citations explicitly establish.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
